Here are both approaches:

## 1. Passing the Product ID as a query string

```csharp
// ProductListPage.xaml.cs or ViewModel
async void OnProductSelected(Product product)
{
    await Shell.Current.GoToAsync($"ProductDetailsPage?productId={product.Id}");
}
```

## 2. Passing the full Product object

```csharp
async void OnProductSelected(Product product)
{
    var parameters = new ShellNavigationQueryParameters
    {
        { "productId", product.Id.ToString() },
        { "product", product }
    };
    await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("ProductDetailsPage", parameters);
}
```

## Receiving both on the ViewModel with IQueryAttributable

```csharp
public class ProductDetailsViewModel : ObservableObject, IQueryAttributable
{
    private string _productId;
    private Product _product;

    public string ProductId
    {
        get => _productId;
        set => SetProperty(ref _productId, value);
    }

    public Product Product
    {
        get => _product;
        set => SetProperty(ref _product, value);
    }

    public void ApplyQueryAttributes(IDictionary<string, object> query)
    {
        // Simple string parameter (from query string or dictionary)
        if (query.TryGetValue("productId", out var id))
            ProductId = id.ToString();

        // Complex object (only available via ShellNavigationQueryParameters)
        if (query.TryGetValue("product", out var obj) && obj is Product product)
            Product = product;
    }
}
```

## Route registration (in AppShell constructor)

```csharp
public AppShell()
{
    InitializeComponent();
    Routing.RegisterRoute("ProductDetailsPage", typeof(ProductDetailsPage));
}
```

**Key differences:**
- **Query string** — serializes to the URI; works for simple types only.
- **ShellNavigationQueryParameters** — passes objects by reference in-memory; supports complex types without serialization.

Both arrive in the same `ApplyQueryAttributes` dictionary, so your ViewModel handles them uniformly.
